<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1175–<a href="/years/1218">1218</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Otto of Brunswick · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto IV, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welf dynasty</a>, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1198 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 1209. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> and nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a>, he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Staufer</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a> for the crown and later broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a>. His defeat with his English and Flemish allies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a> in 1214 ended his power, and Frederick II replaced him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-iv-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otto Posse (1847-1921) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ottta4Brunsvicky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto was the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>, and Matilda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He grew up in England in the care of his grandfather, King Henry II, and became the foster son of his uncle, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a>. In 1190 Richard appointed him Earl of York, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> doubted the grant and prevented him from taking possession. In 1196 Richard, as Duke of Aquitaine, gave him the county of Poitou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of Emperor Henry VI most of the southern princes elected his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a> king in March 1198. The opponents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, on Richard's initiative, chose Otto, who was elected at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> on 9 June 1198 and crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> on 12 July. The civil war drew in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a>, who wished to prevent the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> with the Empire, backed Otto. Philip gained ground, and Otto was defeated at Wassenberg in 1206. Philip was murdered on 21 June 1208, Otto was elected by all the princes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> on 11 November 1208, and Innocent crowned him emperor on 4 October 1209.</p><p>Otto then broke his promises to the pope, taking back Ancona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and declaring Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>'s fiefs forfeit when Frederick refused homage. Innocent excommunicated him on 18 November 1210, and in 1211 several princes elected Frederick king. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Otto fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> at Bouvines on 27 July 1214. His army broke when his wounded horse carried him from the field, and in 1215 he was deposed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Otto spent his last years on his estates between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz Mountains</a> and the heathlands. He fell ill at Harliburg Castle in early May 1218 and died on 19 May, after naming his brother Heinrich heir and executor. He married Beatrice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and then Maria of Brabant, and neither marriage produced children. He was the only German king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welf dynasty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count Ferrand was captured at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214, fighting in the coalition of <b>Otto IV</b> and King John of England against Philip II Augustus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1212 the young Frederick II, seeking allies against his rival <b>Otto IV</b>, issued the Golden Bull of Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip II Augustus conquered Normandy from King John of England in 1202–1204 and at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214 routed Emperor <b>Otto IV</b> and his Flemish allies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brunswick, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harzburg">Harzburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1202" aria-label="Brunswick on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4787 359 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1202">What was here in 1202? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
